// ClassifyClusterObjectChanges decides, once, whether something on record
// explains each settled change, and which audit row does (explained_by). A change
// is explained only by an audit row whose action does what changed, for each
// thing that changed (the rules below, as ClassifyGuestStatusChanges does with its
// direction-dependent task lists): an OSD that went down by osd_stop, osd_restart
// or osd_destroy, or by a reboot or shutdown of its host; one that came up by
// osd_start, osd_restart or osd_create; in and out by osd_in, osd_out,
// osd_create, osd_destroy; a pool's settings by an update; an object appearing or
// going by its create or delete; a replication job's field by an update whose
// details name that field. An unrelated row (osd_in beside an OSD that crashed,
// a trigger beside an edit made in the GUI) explains nothing. A change that
// changed several things needs every one of them explained.
//
// A task in Proxmox that Nexara ingested files under the same key and counts
// while it runs: its audit row is made at the start, and an OSD takes minutes to
// appear. A new OSD cannot be keyed by its number in the task that made it (the
// cephcreateosd UPID names the device), so osd_create is matched by the host it
// ran on ("device"). A row that stands for a task must also have a UPID that names
// the object as Proxmox spells it for that task (osd.12, a pool's name), as its
// key does: a task of another object explains nothing. A task that failed
// explains nothing either, where a failed task cannot have made the change: a stop
// that left the OSD running, a start or a restart that did not bring it up, a
// create (which is matched by host alone, and whose leftover — an OSD entry that is
// down and out — is what the History should show). It still explains where a failed
// task can leave a partial change: a restart that stopped the OSD and could not
// start it (down), a destroy that got part-way (down, out, gone), and a pool's
// create, update or delete, which Ceph applies in steps. A create explains an OSD
// only if the OSD is new, its add on record after the task began: an old OSD coming
// up while a create ran on its host is not that create's.
//
// An OSD going down or coming up is also explained by a rolling update that was
// rebooting its host (the job's own audit row is then explained_by): from the
// reboot's start, which must be no more than an hour before the window, to the
// health check that ends it plus 5 minutes for OSDs to start. Draining and
// upgrading do not take an OSD down, and the restore that follows is hours of VM
// moves in which an OSD crash is no part of the update; neither explains one. Up
// and down only, and in and out only as Ceph's own (below): a reboot does not mark
// an OSD out, an admin does.
//
// A consequence can come long after its cause, so a rule marked lagged does not ask
// the cause to reach the object's last sighting, only to have begun within the 30
// minutes before the change was seen: an OSD comes back up minutes after the reboot
// that took it down, and Ceph marks a down OSD out once it has been down for
// mon_osd_down_out_interval (600 s by default). That is the only way a stop or a
// reboot explains an OSD going out — it must have been down for 9 to 15 minutes,
// as an earlier change on record says, not up since, and the cluster not under
// noout when it went out (the change says: the collector reads the flag with the
// OSDs; a noout set on one OSD alone is not reported by Proxmox, so is not seen) —
// and the only
// way a start, a restart or a reboot explains its going back in: as it came up. An
// OSD marked out by someone while it was up, or in again while it stayed up (no
// coming up in the same reading or the two minutes before), is not explained by the
// stop or the start beside it. A replication job's removal is
// lagged too: Proxmox holds a deleted job until it has cleaned up its replicas.
//
// The window: the row was made no earlier than the object was last seen as it was
// (less 2 minutes for how stale that sighting can be) and no later than the
// change was seen (plus 30 seconds of clock skew) — and never more than 30
